# ============================================================
# _redirects: the asset router's rules. First match wins.
# 301 = permanent (search engines move the ranking across).
#
# A path listed in `run_worker_first` in wrangler.toml never
# reaches this file: it is answered by a Worker, and the asset
# router only gets it if that Worker declines. So the two files
# are one mechanism, and the split is exact:
#
#     wrangler.toml    the address that answers
#     this file        every address that used to
#
# Which means an entry here must NOT also be in that list, and
# the addresses this file sends readers TO must all be in it.
# `check-routes.ts` walks both and fails on a loop or a dead end.
#
# ---- one rule per old address, and no splats ----
#
# Everything is written out, including seventeen stage ladders,
# because a redirect is a fact about an address that WAS live and
# that set does not grow: a stage written after this file cannot
# have an `index.html` form, so there is nothing here to keep in
# step with the data. A placeholder rule would be four lines
# instead of twenty-three and would buy that staleness back, and
# it would also be invisible to `check-routes.ts`, which traces
# every source below to whatever finally answers it.
#
# ONE SET OF OLD ADDRESSES IS NOT HERE: `/skills/courses/`. That
# set DOES grow, 845 of them generated out of a Drive folder, so
# `worker.js` answers both spellings instead and `shared/courses.ts`
# says why beside `lessonOf`. Nothing else on this site may borrow
# that argument: it rests on the section being admin-only and
# unlisted, so there is no canonical to split.
#
# ---- the loop this file used to make ----
#
# Cloudflare serves `/about.html` at `/about` and redirects the
# first to the second on its own, so a rule pointing the other way
# bounces for ever:
#
#     /about → (this file) → /about.html → (Cloudflare) → /about → …
#
# which browsers show as ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS. Every rule below
# points from the `.html` spelling to the extensionless one, which
# is the direction Cloudflare itself would go, so there is no
# other side to bounce off.
# ============================================================

